    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my sec+ and im kinda stuck on something. if we're already hashing passwords, why do we even need to add a \"salt\"? doesnt the hash already make the password unreadable anyway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing passwords is indeed a crucial step in securing them, but it's not a one-way street. Think of a salt as a special ingredient that's added to the password before hashing, like a secret spice that makes the hash more unique to that particular password. This is because the same password can be hashed multiple times with the same algorithm, but adding a salt ensures that even if two different users use the same password, their hashes will be different. It's like a game of hide-and-seek among the hashes - the salt helps the hash function to \"hide\" the password in a way that's unique to each user. \n\nIn other words, without a salt, an attacker could use a rainbow table or brute-force attack to guess a password, but with a salt, they'd be trying to match a unique hash to a specific password, making it much harder. It's a bit like trying to find a specific grain of sand on a beach - the salt helps to make each hash grain unique, so even if an attacker finds one match, they won't be able to find all the others. \n\nNow, I know what you're thinking - \"But what about the hash itself? Doesn't that make the password unreadable?\"",
